EXT. BACKYARD - CHICKEN COOP - AFTERNOON

A leafy suburban yard in late summer. A hand-built chicken coop painted lemon yellow, tin-roofed, with a crooked sign: "THE HEN HOUSE." Paper streamers sag from a clothesline. A card table holds a half-eaten cake.

BEATRIX "BEA" LOMBARDI (8, gap-toothed, tiara over frizzy curls) squats in front of the coop, holding a speckled hen in both arms like a baby.

Her older brother THEO LOMBARDI (12, headphones around his neck, deliberately bored) leans against the fence. Their mother, JUNE LOMBARDI (40s, apron, flour on her cheek), carries out a tray of lemonade.

JUNE
Bea, put Penelope down. She's not a doll.

BEA
She likes it.

THEO
She's literally about to peck your eye.

BEA
She is not!

The hen flaps. Bea squeals and drops her. Penelope lands in the dirt, ruffles, struts away with great dignity.

JUNE
Theo, be nice. It's her birthday.

THEO
I am nice. I came outside.

JUNE
That's the minimum, honey.

The gate clicks. A tall man in a straw hat strides in, a wrapped box under one arm. GRANDPA OSWALDO (70s, big white mustache, suspenders) beams across the yard.

BEA
Grandpa!

OSWALDO
Where is my birthday girl?

BEA
^Where is my present?

OSWALDO
Where is my birthday girl?

The two speak over each other, gleeful, and collapse into laughter. June sets down the tray and shakes her head.

JUNE
Papa, you're early.

OSWALDO
I brought a surprise. Open it, Bea.

Bea tears the paper. Inside, a tiny wooden sign reading "BEA'S COOP." Her eyes shine.

BEA
Grandpa, it's perfect.

OSWALDO
I carved it myself. Terrible carpenter, but good heart.

THEO
(muttering)
Great, now we're all going to cry.

He is already wiping his nose.

The gate clicks again. MRS. PETRAKIS (70s, next-door neighbor, enormous sun hat, a pie held out like an offering) shuffles in.

MRS. PETRAKIS
I smelled cake from my kitchen. I brought apple.

June pulls out her phone to photograph the moment. The hen returns, pecks at the cake, and nobody stops her.

BEA
Penelope, no! That's for the guests!

Penelope looks up, frosting on her beak. Everyone laughs. The afternoon goes golden.
